+# Blocked: the `test_sla` runtime target is not physically achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words(text, 10)` to process the
+reference corpus in **under 1 ms** (`elapsed < 0.001`).
+
+The corpus built by `test_wordfreq.corpus()` is **4,083,137 characters** (about 4 MB)
+containing **600,000 whitespace-separated tokens**.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The SLA target is below the cost of the simplest operation the function must perform.
+Measured on this machine with best-of-15 timings (minimum over runs, so these are
+optimistic lower bounds):
+
+| Operation | Best time |
+|---|---|
+| `text.lower()` only | **1.46 ms** |
+| `text.split()` only | 18.53 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` | 20.86 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 61.44 ms |
+| `Counter(text.lower().split())` | 56.58 ms |
+
+Case-folding the input alone takes ~1.5x the entire SLA budget. It produces a new
+4 MB string and touches every character; no counting or tokenization has happened
+yet. There is therefore no pure-Python implementation of `top_words` that can meet
+this bound, regardless of algorithm. Any implementation must at minimum read the
+full input and lowercase it.
+
+This is not a slow-machine artifact of a marginal miss: the lower bound is already
+larger than the budget, and the full operation is roughly 100x the budget. Even a
+native implementation would have to lowercase, tokenize, and hash-count 600,000
+tokens within 1 ms (~600M tokens/s), which is beyond reach for this workload.
+
+## What was fixed instead
+
+The original implementation was independently slow due to an O(n * vocab) algorithm:
+for every token it scanned the `seen` list (`w in seen`) and called `seen.index(w)`.
+On the reference corpus it took ~6.5 s.
+
+`top_words` now uses `collections.Counter` plus a single sort:
+
+- original: ~6.5 s on the reference corpus
+- optimized: ~0.11 s on the same corpus (~60x faster)
+
+Word extraction, case-insensitivity, tie-breaking, and return shape are unchanged.
+`test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, and `test_ties_alphabetical` pass, and the
+correctness assertion in `test_sla` (the result equals the expected `Counter` result)
+also passes. Only the `< 1 ms` timing assertion fails.
+
+## What was not done
+
+The test was not modified, skipped, or weakened, and no caching, timing manipulation,
+or special-casing of the test input was added. Doing so would hide a real SLA that
+the search team needs to renegotiate.
+
+## Suggested resolution
+
+The search team must either:
+
+1. Relax the SLA to a realistic value (the optimized implementation is ~0.11 s for
+   the 4 MB corpus on this machine), or
+2. Define the SLA for a much smaller input, or
+3. Accept a native extension/service and re-derive the target from its measured
+   throughput — while still accounting for the ~1.5 ms floor of case-folding this
+   corpus.
